.SH NOTES
.BR echo ","
.BR \%noecho ","
.BR \%halfdelay ","
.BR \%intrflush ","
.BR meta ","
.BR nl ","
.BR nonl ","
.BR \%nodelay ","
.BR \%notimeout ","
.BR \%noqiflush ","
.BR \%qiflush ","
.BR \%timeout ","
and
.B \%wtimeout
may be implemented as macros.
.PP
.B \%noraw
and
.B \%nocbreak
follow historical practice in that they attempt to restore
the terminal's canonical (\*(``cooked\*('') line discipline
from raw and cbreak,
respectively.
Mixing
.BR \%raw / noraw
calls with
.BR \%cbreak / nocbreak
calls leads to terminal driver control states that are hard to predict
or understand;
doing so is not recommended.
.PP
.I curses
documentation uses the terms \*(``delay\*('' and \*(``timeout\*(''
freely to describe two related but distinct aspects of input handling,
at the risk of confusing the user.
The functions
.BR \%halfdelay ","
.BR \%nodelay ","
.BR \%timeout ","
and
.B \%wtimeout
configure whether the input character reading function
\%(\fBwgetch\fP(3X) or \fB\%wget_wch\fP(3X))
waits for keyboard input to begin,
and for how long.
.B \%keypad
configures whether that function waits for further input
if the first character it reads is an ambiguous prefix
(like ESC).
Calling
.BR \%notimeout ","
which has nothing to do with
.B \%timeout
or
.BR \%wtimeout ","
makes this delay in expectation of further characters
effectively infinite.
X/Open Curses affords no means of otherwise configuring
the length of this second delay,
but an AIX and
.I \%ncurses
extension,
.BR \%ESCDELAY ,
is available both as an environment variable and a global symbol
permitting the user and application,
respectively,
to do so;
see \fB\%ncurses\fP(3X) and \fB\%curs_variables\fP(3X).
.PP
When
.B \%keypad
is first enabled for a window,
.I \%ncurses
loads the standard function key string capabilities
for the terminal type description of its screen;
see the table entries containing \*(``key_\*('' in \fB\%terminfo\fP(5).
If that description includes extended string capabilities
(produced by the
.B \-x
option of \fB\%@TIC@\fP(1M),
for example),
then for each extended function key
whose capability code begins with \*(``k\*('',
.I \%ncurses
generates a numeric key code.
These code values are affected
by previous loads of terminal type descriptions,
and may differ from one execution of the application to the next.
In
.IR \%ncurses ","
\fB\%keyname\fP(3X) recognizes these generated key codes
and returns a name beginning with \*(``k\*('' denoting the extended
.I \%term\%info
capability code rather than one starting with \*(``KEY_\*('',
which standard
.I curses
key names use.
Further,
an application can use \fB\%define_key\fP(3X) to bind any key code
to a string of the programmer's choice.
This feature enables an application
to check for a function key's presence with \fB\%tigetstr\fP(3X),
and reassign the key code to match its own needs.
.PP
Applications employing the lower-level
.I \%term\%info
API can use \fB\%tigetstr\fP(3X)
to obtain the contents of any string capability,
including extended ones.
Applications employing the higher-level
.I curses
API use an input character reading function
to obtain key codes from input;
the result can vary
depending upon the order in which string capabilities are loaded.
Multiple key capability strings can have the same value,
but the input character reading function can report only one key code.
Most
.I curses
implementations
(including
.IR \%ncurses )
load key definitions in the order
they appear in the
.B \%strfnames
array of string capability names;
see \fB\%term_variables\fP(3X).
.\" ncurses/tinfo/parse_entry.c:lookup_fullname, I think --GBR
The
.I last
capability read using a particular definition
determines the key code to be reported.
.I \%ncurses
loads extended capabilities after standard ones.
Thus,
if an extended string capability's value
is the same as one previously loaded,
the input character reading function
returns the last matching extended one.
